  "measured_charset": "\n (),-./0123456789:=ABCDEFHILMNPRSTUY_abcdefghijklmnoprstuvwxy{}。一主二五價元六加十千及善四增年改於營物現由百的等至萬要資運金零，",
  "notes": {
    "float_formatting": "Excel cell values reach the model through str(), so 1e-7 becomes the STRING \"1e-07\" and 1e16 becomes \"1e+16\". JavaScript's String() gives \"1e-7\" and \"10000000000000000\". The port must implement Python's repr(float): shortest round-trip, a two-digit signed exponent, and a forced .0 on an integral float. 0.30000000000000004 agrees between the two languages and is in the fixture alongside the two that do not.",
    "font_advances": "Pillow measures hinted, grid-fit advances (raqm absent, Layout.BASIC, FT_LOAD_DEFAULT). On Arial at 9pt, 21 of the 95 printable ASCII glyphs differ from round(hmtx): C D F H J M N R T U W Z c k m s v w x y z. The vendored Noto Sans subset has zero such glyphs, so the portable cases alone cannot catch a port that computes advances instead of shipping the table. Both variants record advances_px, unhinted_px and round_unhinted_mismatches per glyph.",
    "hidden_column_range": "read_excel_sheet/hidden-col-range reads the HiddenRange sheet, whose sole feature is <col min=3 max=5 hidden=1>. openpyxl collapses that into one ColumnDimension keyed 'C', so the Python hides column 3 only and reports five headers and '1 hidden column'; the port hides 3..5 and should report three headers and '3 hidden columns'. The sheet exists to keep that the ONE place a TypeScript output is expected to differ -- while the range sat on the Data sheet, twelve cases inherited it and this claim was wrong.",
    "rounding": "Every round() in the office layer is banker's rounding on the binary double, not Math.round. It governs shape dimensions (office.py:798), fill_ratio and used_height_pt (text_metrics.py:321-326) and every pdf_tables coordinate. The goldens record the Python value; they are not re-rounded, because re-rounding would hide exactly the bug they exist to catch.",
    "shape_type_strings": "str(shape.shape_type) comes from python-pptx's MSO_SHAPE_TYPE.__str__ and is recorded verbatim: \"TEXT_BOX (17)\", \"PLACEHOLDER (14)\", \"LINE (9)\" and so on. The port ships this as a lookup table.",
    "vertical_tab": "An a:br inside a paragraph surfaces in shape.text as \\u000b, which is what read_pptx returns.",
    "write_excel_cell_round_trip": "The Python round-trips the workbook through openpyxl, which drops cached formula values and any chart or pivot part. The projection's formula_cells_losing_cached_v records that loss. chart_parts and pivot_parts sit alongside it but prove nothing here: databook.xlsx is hand-assembled and has neither, so both lists are empty before and after. Chart and pivot preservation -- one of the three things the port's in-place edit exists to fix -- has no coverage."
  },
  "schema_version": 1
}
